    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on my thesis regarding the Maillard reaction in high-heat protein cooking. Could you explain the specific role that amino acids and reducing sugars play in the initial stages of the reaction? Also, I'm looking for some detail on how modulating the pH levels of the environment affects the overall rate of the browning process.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the initial stages of the Maillard reaction, free amino acids act as nucleophiles by attacking the carbonyl group of reducing sugars to form unstable N-substituted glycosylamines, which then undergo the rearrangement into Amadori or Heyns products. Modulating the pH significantly impacts this process because a more acidic environment generally accelerates the reaction by promoting the enolization of the sugar intermediate, whereas a basic environment can divert the pathway toward other side reactions or degrade amino acids. Consequently, optimal browning typically occurs at a slightly acidic to neutral pH, as extreme alkalinity may inhibit the necessary complex formation while acidity avoids the degradation of flavor precursors. Ensuring proper pH control is crucial for achieving the desired crust color and flavor profile in high-heat protein cooking.",
      "source": "target",
