    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so you mentioned that extreme alkalinity can divert the pathway or degrade the amino acids... can you dive deeper into that? specifically, what is actually happening at the molecular level when the pH is too high, and does it cause the proteins to break down in a way that changes the texture of the meat too?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At high pH, the amino groups on amino acids become deprotonated (unprotonated), reducing their nucleophilicity and preventing them from bonding with sugars to form Amadori products effectively, while also catalyzing degradation pathways that break down aromatic amino acids into bitter aldehydes like hydantoins. This molecular disruption of the reaction interface doesn't primarily cause chemical denaturation of muscle proteins used as flavor sources, but extreme alkalinity does significantly alter meat texture by excessive softening and eventual breakdown of structural collagen and myofibrillar proteins, which results in a mushy rather than firm crust. These chemical textural changes occur because the surface proteins swell and unfold prematurely before the matrix can properly cross-link during cooking, rather than from direct amino acid destruction.",
